data runner_microvm_cell_readiness_store_disposition: Disposition = SingleAuthority

// THE ON-DISK REALIZATION OF gunbc.runner_microvm_cell_readiness's STORE.
//
// That module owns what a record MEANS -- the (cell, generation) unit, the in-flight arm, the
// monotone write rule, the two withdrawal grains. This module owns the one thing it deliberately
// does not: how a record becomes bytes on a host and back. DESIGN section 3 separates interface
// from realization, and a codec authored inside the semantic module would make every future
// consumer of the semantics a consumer of one encoding.
//
// EVERY DECODE FAILURE IS StoreUnreadable, AND THAT IS THE FAIL-CLOSED DIRECTION RATHER THAN A
// CONVENIENCE. A record this module cannot read is not a record that says Ready and is not a record
// that is absent: it is a store whose evidence nobody may rest an admission on, which is exactly
// what StoreUnreadable withdraws at HOST grain. Decoding "as much as parsed" and dropping the rest
// would turn a corrupt store into a store with fewer cells in it, and every missing cell would then
// read as CellNeverSettled -- a refusal, but at the wrong grain and with the wrong remedy.

// THE STORE IS A DIRECTORY OF ONE FILE PER CELL, AND THAT SHAPE IS THE WHOLE ANSWER TO A DEFECT
// A REVIEW FOUND IN ITS FIRST CUT (review 69037).
//
// THE DEFECT. The store was ONE FILE per host, read-modify-written: read every record, fold the new
// one in, rewrite the document. But a controller is the MainPID of a SLOT unit and a host runs many
// slots, so concurrent writers on one host are the ordinary case and not an exotic one. Two
// controllers reading before either writes means the second rewrites the first's cell FROM ITS OWN
// STALE SNAPSHOT. The reachable direction is the one this whole lane exists to prevent: cell C's
// controller quarantines C, a sibling controller that read before that write rewrites the document,
// and C reverts to a settled CellReady -- a dirty cell readmitted, with no refusal anywhere. The
// readback could not see it either, because it confirmed only that the WRITER'S OWN record came
// back.
//
// WHY ONE FILE PER CELL RATHER THAN A LOCK. A lock makes the bad write refuse; this makes it
// UNWRITABLE, which DESIGN 5 ranks above it -- a writer never holds another cell's bytes, so there
// is nothing to lose. It is also the shape that matches who writes what: the store is read
// HOST-WIDE, because the host-grain soundness checks need every record, and written PER CELL,
// because a cell's record has exactly one writer, the controller of that cell's slot. A lock would
// have matched the write scope to the read scope instead, and it would have introduced the failure
// this lane is about: a controller that dies holding it wedges every other slot on the host until
// something reclaims it, which needs a lease, a clock and a stale-hold protocol -- machinery with
// its own races, bought to guard a critical section that does not have to exist.
//
// DIVERGENCE, STATED (DESIGN 3b's middle value). The conformance row for leasing/locking names
// std.durable_compare_and_set and std.durable_exclusive_hold as the homes for exclusive use, and
// this module inhabits neither. The reason is that after this change there is no cross-cell critical
// section to hold: the shared mutable document is gone. What remains is a read-then-act window on
// the ADMISSION -- a controller admits its cell from a store that another controller may make
// unsound a moment later -- and that window is bounded rather than closed: it can only affect the
// admitting controller's OWN cell, whose evidence it read itself, and the teardown readback re-reads
// the store before settling. Closing it would need the hold, and the hold needs the lease.
//
// THE DIRECTORY IS ROOT'S, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES THE RECORDS ROOT'S. A store any tenant can write is
// a store any tenant can mark Ready. Ownership is observed on the DIRECTORY rather than on each
// file, because a directory no one else may write is a directory in which no one else may create,
// replace or rename a record, whatever a single file's own mode happens to say.
// DECLARED GAP (DESIGN 4b(2), no untracked stall): THIS MODULE'S FILESYSTEM I/O HAS NO WET RECEIPT.
//
// The codec, the cell-to-file addressing and every refusal are covered hermetically, and the
// one-writer-one-cell property is STRUCTURAL -- persist_one_record writes record_of_write's single
// record to the path that write's own cell names, so there is no expression through which it could
// hold another cell's bytes. What is NOT established is that the directory read and the record write
// work against a real filesystem: reading the store, listing it, writing one record and reading it
// back have never executed.
//
// WHY NOT SIMPLY WRITE THE WET CLAIM. The only path this module can address is the production store
// root below, so a wet claim would have to create or destroy a real host's store to run. On a host
// where the microVM path is live that is a claim that breaks the thing it tests, and a guard that
// skipped when a store already existed would be a claim that silently does not run exactly where it
// matters most.
//
// TRIGGER, at capability grain: a store ROOT carried as a value rather than fixed here, the shape
// std.artifact_store's MaterializationStoreRoot already uses -- one production row for the real root
// and a caller-supplied root for a receipt, so the wet claim addresses a directory it owns. SUFFICIENT
// FOR deleting this row. NOT satisfied by a wet claim against the production path, whatever guards it
// carries.
data cell_readiness_store_filesystem_receipt_gap: DissolutionCondition = unbound_dissolution(
  description: "gunbc.runner_microvm_cell_readiness_store reaches the filesystem -- read_cell_readiness_store lists and reads, persist_one_record writes and reads back -- and none of it has executed against a real filesystem, because the only addressable path is the production store root. TRIGGER: the store root carried as a value (the std.artifact_store MaterializationStoreRoot shape), so a receipt can address a directory it owns. SUFFICIENT FOR deleting this row. NOT satisfied by a wet claim against the production path." as NonEmptyStr,
)

data cell_readiness_store_dir: String = "/var/lib/gunbc/microvm-cell-readiness"

data cell_record_suffix: String = ".json"

// A CELL NAMES ITS OWN FILE, SO A CELL WHOSE NAME IS NOT A FILENAME IS REFUSED RATHER THAN JOINED.
// The path is derived from the cell id; a separator in that id would address a record outside the
// store, and "." or ".." would address the directory itself. This is the same refusal
// gunbc.runner_microvm_attempt attempt_segment makes for an attempt id, for the same reason and at
// the same grain: the identity is what every path is derived from, so it is checked where it is
// turned into one.
type CellRecordPath
  = CellRecordAt { path: String }
  | CellRecordNameRefused { spelling: String }

fn cell_record_path(cell: CellId) -> CellRecordPath {
  let name = cell as String
  if name == "." || name == ".." {
    CellRecordNameRefused { spelling: name }
  } else if string_contains(s: name, pattern: "/") {
    CellRecordNameRefused { spelling: name }
  } else {
    CellRecordAt { path: join([cell_readiness_store_dir, "/", name, cell_record_suffix], "") }
  }
}

// THE WIRE NAME OF A FACT IS product.fabric.sanitation's OWN LABEL, not a second spelling minted
// here. The decoder is the structural inverse of that projection: it walks the closed fact list and
// matches the label each one renders to, so adding an eighth fact makes the decoder cover it with
// no edit, and a label this module invented could never disagree with the one an operator reads.
fn sanitation_facts_all() -> List<SanitationFact> {
  [
    NoSurvivingProcesses,
    CgroupEmpty,
    MountsRemoved,
    NetworkNamespaceRemoved,
    SlotNetworkQuiescent,
    WritableLayersDeleted,
    SecretsRemoved,
  ]
}

fn decode_sanitation_fact(label: String) -> SanitationFact? {
  filter(sanitation_facts_all(), f => sanitation_fact_label(fact: f) == label).first()
}

// ONE REFUSAL CARRIER FOR THE WHOLE CODEC, so a nested failure travels outward with the reason it
// failed rather than collapsing into an absent optional at each level. A `T?` return at every step
// would make "the cell key was missing" and "the readiness object was malformed" the same value at
// the top, and the reason is the entire remedy.
type Decoded<T>
  = DecodeOk { value: T }
  | DecodeRefused { reason: NonEmptyStr }

fn decoded_string(obj: JsonValue, key: String) -> Decoded<String> {
  match json_field_string(obj: obj, key: key) {
    FieldRead { value: v } => DecodeOk { value: v }
    FieldAbsent => DecodeRefused { reason: join(["a record has no `", key, "` member"], "") as NonEmptyStr }
    FieldMalformed { cause: c } => DecodeRefused { reason: join(["a record's `", key, "` member is unreadable: ", c], "") as NonEmptyStr }
  }
}

fn decoded_nonempty(obj: JsonValue, key: String) -> Decoded<NonEmptyStr> {
  match decoded_string(obj: obj, key: key) {
    DecodeRefused { reason: r } => DecodeRefused { reason: r }
    DecodeOk { value: v } =>
      if v == "" {
        DecodeRefused { reason: join(["a record's `", key, "` member is empty"], "") as NonEmptyStr }
      } else {
        DecodeOk { value: v as NonEmptyStr }
      }
  }
}

fn decoded_int(obj: JsonValue, key: String) -> Decoded<Int> {
  match json_field_int(obj: obj, key: key) {
    FieldRead { value: v } => DecodeOk { value: v }
    FieldAbsent => DecodeRefused { reason: join(["a record has no `", key, "` member"], "") as NonEmptyStr }
    FieldMalformed { cause: c } => DecodeRefused { reason: join(["a record's `", key, "` member is unreadable: ", c], "") as NonEmptyStr }
  }
}

fn decoded_object(obj: JsonValue, key: String) -> Decoded<JsonValue> {
  match json_field(obj: obj, key: key) {
    FieldRead { value: v } => DecodeOk { value: v }
    FieldAbsent => DecodeRefused { reason: join(["a record has no `", key, "` member"], "") as NonEmptyStr }
    FieldMalformed { cause: c } => DecodeRefused { reason: join(["a record's `", key, "` member is unreadable: ", c], "") as NonEmptyStr }
  }
}

fn decoded_array_elements(v: JsonValue) -> Decoded<List<JsonValue>> {
  match v {
    JsonArray { elements: es } => DecodeOk { value: es }
    JsonNull => DecodeRefused { reason: "a member that must be an array is null" as NonEmptyStr }
    JsonBool { value: _ } => DecodeRefused { reason: "a member that must be an array is a boolean" as NonEmptyStr }
    JsonNumber { lexeme: _ } => DecodeRefused { reason: "a member that must be an array is a number" as NonEmptyStr }
    JsonString { value: _ } => DecodeRefused { reason: "a member that must be an array is a string" as NonEmptyStr }
    JsonObject { members: _ } => DecodeRefused { reason: "a member that must be an array is an object" as NonEmptyStr }
  }
}

// --- the fact / observation / unproven codec ---

fn encode_fact(fact: SanitationFact) -> JsonValue {
  json_string(s: sanitation_fact_label(fact: fact))
}

fn decoded_fact(obj: JsonValue, key: String) -> Decoded<SanitationFact> {
  match decoded_string(obj: obj, key: key) {
    DecodeRefused { reason: r } => DecodeRefused { reason: r }
    DecodeOk { value: label } =>
      match decode_sanitation_fact(label: label) {
        Present { value: f } => DecodeOk { value: f }
        Absent => DecodeRefused { reason: join(["`", label, "` is not a sanitation fact this corpus declares"], "") as NonEmptyStr }
      }
  }
}

data observation_verdict_key: String = "verdict"
data observation_confirmed_tag: String = "confirmed"
data observation_refuted_tag: String = "refuted"
data observation_unobservable_tag: String = "unobservable"

fn encode_observation(o: SanitationObservation) -> JsonValue {
  match o {
    FactConfirmed { fact: f } =>
      json_object(members: [
        json_kv(key: observation_verdict_key, value: json_string(s: observation_confirmed_tag)),
        json_kv(key: "fact", value: encode_fact(fact: f)),
      ])
    FactRefuted { fact: f, detail: d } =>
      json_object(members: [
        json_kv(key: observation_verdict_key, value: json_string(s: observation_refuted_tag)),
        json_kv(key: "fact", value: encode_fact(fact: f)),
        json_kv(key: "detail", value: json_string(s: d as String)),
      ])
    FactUnobservable { fact: f, reason: r } =>
      json_object(members: [
        json_kv(key: observation_verdict_key, value: json_string(s: observation_unobservable_tag)),
        json_kv(key: "fact", value: encode_fact(fact: f)),
        json_kv(key: "reason", value: json_string(s: r as String)),
      ])
  }
}

fn decode_observation(v: JsonValue) -> Decoded<SanitationObservation> {
  match decoded_fact(obj: v, key: "fact") {
    DecodeRefused { reason: r } => DecodeRefused { reason: r }
    DecodeOk { value: f } =>
      match decoded_string(obj: v, key: observation_verdict_key) {
        DecodeRefused { reason: r } => DecodeRefused { reason: r }
        DecodeOk { value: verdict } => decode_observation_of_verdict(v: v, fact: f, verdict: verdict)
      }
  }
}

fn decode_observation_of_verdict(v: JsonValue, fact: SanitationFact, verdict: String) -> Decoded<SanitationObservation> {
  if verdict == observation_confirmed_tag {
    DecodeOk { value: FactConfirmed { fact: fact } }
  } else if verdict == observation_refuted_tag {
    match decoded_nonempty(obj: v, key: "detail") {
      DecodeRefused { reason: r } => DecodeRefused { reason: r }
      DecodeOk { value: d } => DecodeOk { value: FactRefuted { fact: fact, detail: d } }
    }
  } else if verdict == observation_unobservable_tag {
    match decoded_nonempty(obj: v, key: "reason") {
      DecodeRefused { reason: r } => DecodeRefused { reason: r }
      DecodeOk { value: rr } => DecodeOk { value: FactUnobservable { fact: fact, reason: rr } }
    }
  } else {
    DecodeRefused { reason: join(["`", verdict, "` is not one of the three sanitation verdicts"], "") as NonEmptyStr }
  }
}

data unproven_kind_key: String = "kind"
data unproven_not_observed_tag: String = "not_observed"
data unproven_observed_failing_tag: String = "observed_failing"

fn encode_unproven(u: UnprovenFact) -> JsonValue {
  match u {
    FactNotObserved { fact: f } =>
      json_object(members: [
        json_kv(key: unproven_kind_key, value: json_string(s: unproven_not_observed_tag)),
        json_kv(key: "fact", value: encode_fact(fact: f)),
      ])
    FactObservedFailing { observation: o } =>
      json_object(members: [
        json_kv(key: unproven_kind_key, value: json_string(s: unproven_observed_failing_tag)),
        json_kv(key: "observation", value: encode_observation(o: o)),
      ])
  }
}

fn decode_unproven(v: JsonValue) -> Decoded<UnprovenFact> {
  match decoded_string(obj: v, key: unproven_kind_key) {
    DecodeRefused { reason: r } => DecodeRefused { reason: r }
    DecodeOk { value: kind } =>
      if kind == unproven_not_observed_tag {
        match decoded_fact(obj: v, key: "fact") {
          DecodeRefused { reason: r } => DecodeRefused { reason: r }
          DecodeOk { value: f } => DecodeOk { value: FactNotObserved { fact: f } }
        }
      } else if kind == unproven_observed_failing_tag {
        match decoded_object(obj: v, key: "observation") {
          DecodeRefused { reason: r } => DecodeRefused { reason: r }
          DecodeOk { value: ov } =>
            match decode_observation(v: ov) {
              DecodeRefused { reason: r } => DecodeRefused { reason: r }
              DecodeOk { value: o } => DecodeOk { value: FactObservedFailing { observation: o } }
            }
        }
      } else {
        DecodeRefused { reason: join(["`", kind, "` is not one of the two unproven-fact shapes"], "") as NonEmptyStr }
      }
  }
}

// A LIST DECODE IS ALL-OR-REFUSED. The fold carries the first refusal outward instead of skipping
// the element it could not read: a quarantine that loses one unproven fact is a quarantine with a
// shorter list, and a shorter list is exactly what a reader would take as progress.
fn decode_unproven_step(acc: Decoded<List<UnprovenFact>>, v: JsonValue) -> Decoded<List<UnprovenFact>> {
  match acc {
    DecodeRefused { reason: r } => DecodeRefused { reason: r }
    DecodeOk { value: xs } =>
      match decode_unproven(v: v) {
        DecodeRefused { reason: r } => DecodeRefused { reason: r }
        DecodeOk { value: u } => DecodeOk { value: concat(xs, [u]) }
      }
  }
}

fn no_unproven_yet() -> Decoded<List<UnprovenFact>> {
  DecodeOk { value: [] as List<UnprovenFact> }
}

fn decode_unproven_list(elements: List<JsonValue>) -> Decoded<List<UnprovenFact>> {
  fold(elements, init: no_unproven_yet(), f: (acc, v) => decode_unproven_step(acc: acc, v: v))
}

// --- the readiness codec ---

data readiness_state_key: String = "state"
data readiness_ready_tag: String = "ready"
data readiness_quarantined_tag: String = "quarantined"

fn encode_readiness(r: CellReadiness) -> JsonValue {
  match r {
    CellReady { cell: c } =>
      json_object(members: [
        json_kv(key: readiness_state_key, value: json_string(s: readiness_ready_tag)),
        json_kv(key: "cell", value: json_string(s: c as String)),
      ])
    CellQuarantined { cell: c, unproven: u } =>
      json_object(members: [
        json_kv(key: readiness_state_key, value: json_string(s: readiness_quarantined_tag)),
        json_kv(key: "cell", value: json_string(s: c as String)),
        json_kv(key: "unproven", value: json_array(elements: map(u, x => encode_unproven(u: x)))),
      ])
  }
}

// THE READINESS CARRIES ITS OWN CELL AND IT IS DECODED AS WRITTEN, NEVER REPAIRED FROM THE KEY.
// gunbc.runner_microvm_cell_readiness record_incoherence exists precisely to catch a settled record
// whose readiness names a different cell than its key, and a decoder that substituted the key for
// the encoded cell would make that check permanently green by construction -- a wall dismantled from
// underneath by the codec that feeds it (DESIGN 4b: an inert check is worse than an absent one).
fn decode_readiness(v: JsonValue) -> Decoded<CellReadiness> {
  match decoded_nonempty(obj: v, key: "cell") {
    DecodeRefused { reason: r } => DecodeRefused { reason: r }
    DecodeOk { value: c } =>
      match decoded_string(obj: v, key: readiness_state_key) {
        DecodeRefused { reason: r } => DecodeRefused { reason: r }
        DecodeOk { value: state } =>
          if state == readiness_ready_tag {
            DecodeOk { value: CellReady { cell: c as CellId } }
          } else if state == readiness_quarantined_tag {
            decode_quarantined(v: v, cell: c as CellId)
          } else {
            DecodeRefused { reason: join(["`", state, "` is not a cell-readiness state"], "") as NonEmptyStr }
          }
      }
  }
}

fn decode_quarantined(v: JsonValue, cell: CellId) -> Decoded<CellReadiness> {
  match decoded_object(obj: v, key: "unproven") {
    DecodeRefused { reason: r } => DecodeRefused { reason: r }
    DecodeOk { value: arr } =>
      match decoded_array_elements(v: arr) {
        DecodeRefused { reason: r } => DecodeRefused { reason: r }
        DecodeOk { value: es } =>
          match decode_unproven_list(elements: es) {
            DecodeRefused { reason: r } => DecodeRefused { reason: r }
            DecodeOk { value: us } => DecodeOk { value: CellQuarantined { cell: cell, unproven: us } }
          }
      }
  }
}

// --- the in-flight attempt codec ---

// THE ATTEMPT IS PERSISTED AS ITS CONSTRUCTOR'S INPUTS AND REBUILT THROUGH THAT CONSTRUCTOR.
// MicroVmAttempt is sealed and its segment refuses traversal (gunbc.runner_microvm_attempt); a
// decoder that assembled the record directly would be a second constructor and would let a store
// edited by hand reintroduce `../shared` as an attempt id. So the bytes carry host, slot index and
// the segment's spelling, and microvm_attempt is asked to accept them exactly as the live path does.
fn encode_attempt(a: InFlightAttempt) -> JsonValue {
  json_object(members: [
    json_kv(key: "host", value: json_string(s: a.attempt.slot.host as String)),
    json_kv(key: "slot_index", value: json_int(n: a.attempt.slot.slot_index as Int)),
    json_kv(key: "attempt_id", value: json_string(s: a.attempt.attempt_id.value as String)),
    json_kv(key: "invocation_id", value: json_string(s: a.invocation_id as String)),
    json_kv(key: "jit_registration_id", value: json_string(s: a.jit_registration_id as String)),
    json_kv(key: "credential_device_path", value: json_string(s: a.credential_device_path)),
  ])
}

// THE SLOT INDEX CROSSES Int TO Nat THROUGH std.checked_arithmetic, NOT THROUGH A CAST, and the
// first cut of this decoder used the cast and FAILED at runtime on every in-flight record. `as Nat`
// in a record-field position typechecks and refuses only when it EVALUATES (the crossing is admitted
// at a function RETURN position and not inside a record literal), so a decoder written that way is
// green on every typecheck and aborts mid-run on the first store it reads. The negative guard above
// stays: checked_int_magnitude returns a MAGNITUDE, so it would turn -1 into slot 1 rather than
// refusing, and a record naming a slot that does not exist must not become a record naming one that
// does.
fn decode_attempt(v: JsonValue) -> Decoded<InFlightAttempt> {
  match decoded_nonempty(obj: v, key: "host") {
    DecodeRefused { reason: r } => DecodeRefused { reason: r }
    DecodeOk { value: host } =>
      match decoded_int(obj: v, key: "slot_index") {
        DecodeRefused { reason: r } => DecodeRefused { reason: r }
        DecodeOk { value: idx } =>
          if idx < 0 {
            DecodeRefused { reason: "a record's `slot_index` is negative, so it names no slot" as NonEmptyStr }
          } else {
            match checked_int_magnitude(a: idx) {
              CheckedNatOverflow { cause: _ } =>
                DecodeRefused { reason: "a record's `slot_index` is not representable as a slot index" as NonEmptyStr }
              CheckedNatReady { value: n } =>
                decode_attempt_after_slot(v: v, slot: RunnerSlotIdentity { host: host as HostIdentity, slot_index: n })
            }
          }
      }
  }
}

fn decode_attempt_after_slot(v: JsonValue, slot: RunnerSlotIdentity) -> Decoded<InFlightAttempt> {
  match decoded_string(obj: v, key: "attempt_id") {
    DecodeRefused { reason: r } => DecodeRefused { reason: r }
    DecodeOk { value: id } =>
      match microvm_attempt(slot: slot, attempt_id: id) {
        MicroVmAttemptRefused { refusal: ref } =>
          DecodeRefused { reason: join(["a record's attempt id is not a usable segment: ", attempt_segment_refusal_text(r: ref)], "") as NonEmptyStr }
        MicroVmAttemptAccepted { attempt: at } => decode_attempt_after_identity(v: v, attempt: at)
      }
  }
}

fn decode_attempt_after_identity(v: JsonValue, attempt: MicroVmAttempt) -> Decoded<InFlightAttempt> {
  match decoded_nonempty(obj: v, key: "invocation_id") {
    DecodeRefused { reason: r } => DecodeRefused { reason: r }
    DecodeOk { value: inv } =>
      match decoded_nonempty(obj: v, key: "jit_registration_id") {
        DecodeRefused { reason: r } => DecodeRefused { reason: r }
        DecodeOk { value: reg } =>
          match decoded_nonempty(obj: v, key: "credential_device_path") {
            DecodeRefused { reason: r } => DecodeRefused { reason: r }
            DecodeOk { value: dev } =>
              DecodeOk {
                value: InFlightAttempt {
                  attempt: attempt,
                  invocation_id: inv,
                  jit_registration_id: reg,
                  credential_device_path: dev as String,
                },
              }
          }
      }
  }
}

// --- the record codec ---

data record_kind_key: String = "kind"
data record_in_flight_tag: String = "in_flight"
data record_settled_tag: String = "settled"

fn encode_record(r: CellReadinessRecord) -> JsonValue {
  match r {
    CellAttemptInFlight { cell: c, generation: g, attempt: a } =>
      json_object(members: [
        json_kv(key: record_kind_key, value: json_string(s: record_in_flight_tag)),
        json_kv(key: "cell", value: json_string(s: c as String)),
        json_kv(key: "generation", value: json_int(n: g)),
        json_kv(key: "attempt", value: encode_attempt(a: a)),
      ])
    CellSettled { cell: c, generation: g, readiness: rd } =>
      json_object(members: [
        json_kv(key: record_kind_key, value: json_string(s: record_settled_tag)),
        json_kv(key: "cell", value: json_string(s: c as String)),
        json_kv(key: "generation", value: json_int(n: g)),
        json_kv(key: "readiness", value: encode_readiness(r: rd)),
      ])
  }
}

fn decode_record(v: JsonValue) -> Decoded<CellReadinessRecord> {
  match decoded_nonempty(obj: v, key: "cell") {
    DecodeRefused { reason: r } => DecodeRefused { reason: r }
    DecodeOk { value: c } =>
      match decoded_int(obj: v, key: "generation") {
        DecodeRefused { reason: r } => DecodeRefused { reason: r }
        DecodeOk { value: g } => decode_record_of_kind(v: v, cell: c as CellId, generation: g)
      }
  }
}

fn decode_record_of_kind(v: JsonValue, cell: CellId, generation: Int) -> Decoded<CellReadinessRecord> {
  match decoded_string(obj: v, key: record_kind_key) {
    DecodeRefused { reason: r } => DecodeRefused { reason: r }
    DecodeOk { value: kind } =>
      if kind == record_in_flight_tag {
        match decoded_object(obj: v, key: "attempt") {
          DecodeRefused { reason: r } => DecodeRefused { reason: r }
          DecodeOk { value: av } =>
            match decode_attempt(v: av) {
              DecodeRefused { reason: r } => DecodeRefused { reason: r }
              DecodeOk { value: a } => DecodeOk { value: CellAttemptInFlight { cell: cell, generation: generation, attempt: a } }
            }
        }
      } else if kind == record_settled_tag {
        match decoded_object(obj: v, key: "readiness") {
          DecodeRefused { reason: r } => DecodeRefused { reason: r }
          DecodeOk { value: rv } =>
            match decode_readiness(v: rv) {
              DecodeRefused { reason: r } => DecodeRefused { reason: r }
              DecodeOk { value: rd } => DecodeOk { value: CellSettled { cell: cell, generation: generation, readiness: rd } }
            }
        }
      } else {
        DecodeRefused { reason: join(["`", kind, "` is not one of the two cell-readiness record shapes"], "") as NonEmptyStr }
      }
  }
}

// --- the whole document ---

fn no_records_yet() -> Decoded<List<CellReadinessRecord>> {
  DecodeOk { value: [] as List<CellReadinessRecord> }
}

// ONE RECORD IS ONE DOCUMENT, because one record is one file. The array codec this replaced was the
// host-wide document whose rewrite lost other cells' records; there is no whole-store document to
// encode any more, and keeping one would be a second representation of a store that is now a
// directory.
fn encode_record_document(record: CellReadinessRecord) -> String {
  serialize_json(v: encode_record(r: record))
}

fn decode_record_document(text: String) -> Decoded<CellReadinessRecord> {
  match parse_json_document(s: text) {
    JsonDocumentUnreadable { gap: gap } =>
      DecodeRefused { reason: join(["a cell record is ", json_document_gap_text(gap: gap)], "") as NonEmptyStr }
    JsonDocumentParsed { value: doc } => decode_record(v: doc)
  }
}

// THE STORE IS ALL-OR-REFUSED ACROSS ITS FILES, exactly as it was across a document's elements, and
// for the same reason: a store that dropped the one record it could not read would report that cell
// as never settled -- a refusal, but at the wrong grain and with the wrong remedy. The fold carries
// the first refusal outward rather than skipping the file it came from.
fn decode_store_documents(bodies: List<String>) -> Decoded<List<CellReadinessRecord>> {
  fold(bodies, init: no_records_yet(), f: (acc, body) => decode_document_step(acc: acc, body: body))
}

fn decode_document_step(acc: Decoded<List<CellReadinessRecord>>, body: String) -> Decoded<List<CellReadinessRecord>> {
  match acc {
    DecodeRefused { reason: r } => DecodeRefused { reason: r }
    DecodeOk { value: xs } =>
      match decode_record_document(text: body) {
        DecodeRefused { reason: r } => DecodeRefused { reason: r }
        DecodeOk { value: rec } => DecodeOk { value: concat(xs, [rec]) }
      }
  }
}

// --- the wet half: the store as a file on this host ---

// OWNERSHIP IS TWO READINGS AND BOTH ARE REQUIRED. uid 0 alone admits mode 0666, which is a store
// every tenant may write; a restrictive mode alone admits a directory the runner user owns and may
// chmod at will. gunbc.runner_microvm_cell_readiness StoreNotRootOwned carries both because the
// remedy names both, so both are read here rather than folded into one verdict.
//
// THE READING ITSELF IS extdeps.tools.stat's, not this module's. It used to be a StoreStatReading
// declared here -- the same three arms and the same fold that gunbc.runner_attempt_launch had also
// minted for its own stat readback, one concept invented twice in one change (review 69037). The
// fact's layer is the stat boundary both callers already import from.

// A WRITE BIT OUTSIDE ROOT IS READ OFF THE OCTAL DIGIT, not off a whole-mode equality. Pinning the
// mode to one spelling would refuse 0400 -- a store root may read and not write, which is stricter
// and not less safe -- and would call a legitimate tightening a defect. What matters is the one
// fact the store's soundness rests on: nobody but root may write it.
fn octal_digit_grants_write(d: String) -> Bool {
  d == "2" || d == "3" || d == "6" || d == "7"
}

type StoreModeReading
  = ModeAdmitsOnlyRoot
  | ModeAdmitsOthers
  | ModeUnintelligible { detail: String }

fn store_mode_reading(mode: String) -> StoreModeReading {
  let n = mode.length()
  if n < 2 {
    ModeUnintelligible { detail: join(["stat reported the mode as `", mode, "`, which carries no group and other digits"], "") }
  } else if octal_digit_grants_write(d: substring(s: mode, start: n - 2, end: n - 1))
      || octal_digit_grants_write(d: substring(s: mode, start: n - 1, end: n)) {
    ModeAdmitsOthers
  } else {
    ModeAdmitsOnlyRoot
  }
}

type StoreOwnershipReading
  = OwnershipRead { ownership: CellReadinessStoreOwnership }
  | OwnershipUnread { reason: NonEmptyStr }

fn read_store_ownership(path: String) -> StoreOwnershipReading
  uses net: Network
{
  match stat_field_reading(outcome: run_shell_command_capture(command: stat_owner_uid_command(path: path), transport: LocalExec)) {
    StatFieldUnread { detail: d } =>
      OwnershipUnread { reason: join(["the store directory's owner could not be read: ", d], "") as NonEmptyStr }
    StatFieldRead { value: uid_text } =>
      match stat_field_reading(outcome: run_shell_command_capture(command: stat_mode_command(path: path), transport: LocalExec)) {
        StatFieldUnread { detail: d } =>
          OwnershipUnread { reason: join(["the store directory's mode could not be read: ", d], "") as NonEmptyStr }
        StatFieldRead { value: mode_text } => ownership_of_readings(uid_text: uid_text, mode_text: mode_text)
      }
  }
}

fn ownership_of_readings(uid_text: String, mode_text: String) -> StoreOwnershipReading {
  match parse_int(s: uid_text) {
    Absent =>
      OwnershipUnread { reason: join(["stat reported the store's owner as `", uid_text, "`, which is not a uid"], "") as NonEmptyStr }
    Present { value: uid } =>
      match store_mode_reading(mode: mode_text) {
        ModeUnintelligible { detail: d } => OwnershipUnread { reason: d as NonEmptyStr }
        ModeAdmitsOthers =>
          OwnershipRead { ownership: StoreNotRootOwned { owner_uid: uid, mode: mode_text as NonEmptyStr } }
        ModeAdmitsOnlyRoot =>
          if uid == 0 {
            OwnershipRead { ownership: StoreRootOwned }
          } else {
            OwnershipRead { ownership: StoreNotRootOwned { owner_uid: uid, mode: mode_text as NonEmptyStr } }
          }
      }
  }
}

// THE READ IS HOST-WIDE, because host-grain soundness is a property of every record together: a
// duplicate, or a record whose readiness names a different cell than its key, makes the store
// unsound for the cell being asked about too. The store is a directory now, so reading it whole is
// listing that directory and reading each record; the WRITE is the half that narrowed to one cell.
//
// AN ABSENT STORE IS UNREADABLE RATHER THAN EMPTY. "There is no store" and "the store holds no
// records" are different facts with different remedies, and only the second is a reading. A host
// with no store is a host where nothing has ever established a cell's emptiness, so admitting its
// cells from an empty list would hand every cell on a freshly imaged host to the first incarnation
// with no readback behind it. StoreUnreadable withdraws the HOST and names the absence, which sends
// an operator at the one thing that is missing instead of at every cell in turn.
fn read_cell_readiness_store() -> CellReadinessStoreRead
  uses net: Network, fs: Filesystem
{
  match read_store_ownership(path: cell_readiness_store_dir) {
    OwnershipUnread { reason: r } => StoreUnreadable { reason: r }
    OwnershipRead { ownership: own } => read_store_records(ownership: own)
  }
}

// A RECORD FILE THAT CANNOT BE READ REFUSES THE WHOLE STORE, and so does a listing that fails. The
// alternative is a store with fewer cells in it, and every missing cell then reads as never settled
// -- the right direction at the wrong grain, pointing an operator at a cell instead of at the
// store.
fn read_store_records(ownership: CellReadinessStoreOwnership) -> CellReadinessStoreRead
  uses net: Network, fs: Filesystem
{
  let listed = Filesystem.List(path: cell_readiness_store_dir)
  if listed.success == false {
    StoreUnreadable {
      reason: join(["the cell-readiness store at ", cell_readiness_store_dir, " could not be listed: ", listed.error], "") as NonEmptyStr,
    }
  } else {
    match read_record_bodies(names: record_file_names(entries: listed.entries)) {
      BodiesUnread { reason: r } => StoreUnreadable { reason: r }
      BodiesRead { bodies: bodies } =>
        match decode_store_documents(bodies: bodies) {
          DecodeRefused { reason: r } => StoreUnreadable { reason: r }
          DecodeOk { value: records } => StoreRead { ownership: ownership, records: records }
        }
    }
  }
}

// ONLY THE RECORD FILES ARE READ, and an entry that is not one is skipped rather than refused. The
// store's directory is the store's, but an editor's swap file or an operator's note is not a record
// that failed to decode -- refusing on it would take a host out of service for a file nothing reads.
// What is NOT skipped is a file that ends in the record suffix and does not decode: that one claims
// to be a record.
fn record_file_names(entries: String) -> List<String> {
  filter(split(s: entries, delimiter: "\n"), n => ends_with(s: trim(s: n), suffix: cell_record_suffix))
}

type RecordBodies
  = BodiesRead { bodies: List<String> }
  | BodiesUnread { reason: NonEmptyStr }

fn read_record_bodies(names: List<String>) -> RecordBodies
  uses fs: Filesystem
{
  fold(names, init: BodiesRead { bodies: [] as List<String> }, f: (acc, name) => read_body_step(acc: acc, name: trim(s: name)))
}

fn read_body_step(acc: RecordBodies, name: String) -> RecordBodies
  uses fs: Filesystem
{
  match acc {
    BodiesUnread { reason: r } => BodiesUnread { reason: r }
    BodiesRead { bodies: bs } => {
      let path = join([cell_readiness_store_dir, "/", name], "")
      let body = Filesystem.Read(path: path)
      if body.success == false {
        BodiesUnread { reason: join(["the cell record at ", path, " could not be read: ", body.error], "") as NonEmptyStr }
      } else {
        BodiesRead { bodies: concat(bs, [body.content]) }
      }
    }
  }
}

// THE WRITE IS DECIDED BY THE STORE'S OWN AUTHORITY, TOUCHES ONE CELL'S FILE, AND IS THEN READ BACK.
//
// gunbc.runner_microvm_cell_readiness apply_cell_readiness_write decides whether the write may
// happen at all; this module never re-decides it and never writes past a refusal. What it adds is
// the half that authority cannot have: the bytes reaching the disk, and an INDEPENDENT reading that
// they did. A successful Filesystem.Write is the filesystem's claim; the record read back out of the
// re-decoded file is the host's observation of the result, and the two are separated in time by the
// failures -- a full disk, a truncated write -- that make the claim wrong.
//
// THE RESULT OF apply_cell_readiness_write IS CONSUMED FOR ITS VERDICT AND NOT FOR ITS RECORDS. It
// returns the whole next store, which is what the host-wide document used to write; taking those
// bytes is what lost other cells' records. What is written here is record_of_write's single record,
// into the file the write's own cell names -- so a writer holding a stale snapshot of some OTHER
// cell has nothing to write it with.
type CellReadinessStorePersist
  = StorePersisted { record: CellReadinessRecord }
  | StorePersistRefusedByStore { result: CellReadinessWriteResult }
  | StorePersistCellNotAddressable { spelling: String }
  | StorePersistWriteFailed { path: String }
  | StorePersistReadbackFailed { reason: NonEmptyStr }
  | StorePersistReadbackMismatch { path: String }

fn persist_cell_readiness_write(write: CellReadinessWrite) -> CellReadinessStorePersist
  uses net: Network, fs: Filesystem
{
  match read_cell_readiness_store() {
    StoreUnreadable { reason: r } => StorePersistReadbackFailed { reason: r }
    StoreRead { ownership: _, records: records } =>
      match apply_cell_readiness_write(records: records, write: write) {
        WriteAccepted { records: _ } => persist_one_record(write: write)
        refused => StorePersistRefusedByStore { result: refused }
      }
  }
}

fn persist_one_record(write: CellReadinessWrite) -> CellReadinessStorePersist
  uses net: Network, fs: Filesystem
{
  match cell_record_path(cell: write_cell(write: write)) {
    CellRecordNameRefused { spelling: sp } => StorePersistCellNotAddressable { spelling: sp }
    CellRecordAt { path: path } => {
      let record = record_of_write(write: write)
      let wrote = Filesystem.Write(path: path, content: encode_record_document(record: record))
      if wrote.success == false {
        StorePersistWriteFailed { path: path }
      } else {
        confirm_persisted(path: path, record: record)
      }
    }
  }
}

fn record_bytes_equal(a: CellReadinessRecord, b: CellReadinessRecord) -> Bool {
  encode_record_document(record: a) == encode_record_document(record: b)
}

// THE READBACK IS OF THE ONE FILE THAT WAS WRITTEN, and it compares the record rather than merely
// finding one. A file that came back holding a DIFFERENT record is not a missing record: it is a
// write that landed somewhere other than where this one meant it to, or bytes that changed under
// it, and reporting it as absent would send a reader to look for a file that is right there.
fn confirm_persisted(path: String, record: CellReadinessRecord) -> CellReadinessStorePersist
  uses fs: Filesystem
{
  let back = Filesystem.Read(path: path)
  if back.success == false {
    StorePersistReadbackFailed {
      reason: join(["the cell record at ", path, " could not be read back: ", back.error], "") as NonEmptyStr,
    }
  } else {
    match decode_record_document(text: back.content) {
      DecodeRefused { reason: r } => StorePersistReadbackFailed { reason: r }
      DecodeOk { value: seen } =>
        if record_bytes_equal(a: seen, b: record) {
          StorePersisted { record: record }
        } else {
          StorePersistReadbackMismatch { path: path }
        }
    }
  }
}
